 \begin{abstract}
-Data gaps near the onset of black hole ringdown can obscure much of its rapidly decaying signal. We present DenoiseGapFiller (DGF), a neural network that jointly denoises and reconstructs ringdown waveforms using complementary time--frequency and temporal representations. We evaluate DGF on 6,000 independent simulated LISA $A/E$ observations formed by adding stationary Gaussian instrument noise to ringdown signals with gaps spanning approximately 6\%--10\% of each record. For network signal-to-noise ratios $\rho\ge10$, the mean direct noise-weighted mismatch is $5.32\times10^{-3}$ at fixed arrival time and phase. The corresponding mean over all 6,000 test signals is $8.52\times10^{-3}$. Across the full test population, the mean absolute analytic-phase error at each channel's clean-signal envelope peak is below $4^\circ$. These results demonstrate recovery of damped oscillations through intervals containing the signal peak in the simulated source population.
+Data gaps near the onset of black hole ringdown can obscure much of its rapidly decaying signal. We present DenoiseGapFiller (DGF), a neural network that jointly denoises and reconstructs ringdown waveforms using complementary time--frequency and temporal representations. We evaluate DGF on 6,000 independent simulated LISA $A/E$ observations formed by adding stationary Gaussian instrument noise to ringdown signals with gaps spanning approximately 6\%--10\% of each record. For network signal-to-noise ratios $\rho\ge10$, the mean direct noise-weighted mismatch is $5.10\times10^{-3}$ at fixed arrival time and phase. The corresponding mean over all 6,000 test signals is $8.21\times10^{-3}$. Across the full test population, the mean absolute analytic-phase error at each channel's clean-signal envelope peak is below $4^\circ$. These results demonstrate recovery of damped oscillations through intervals containing the signal peak in the simulated source population.
 \end{abstract}
 
